<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phoenice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Foinike, Finiq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phoenice was an ancient Greek city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaonia">Chaonia</a>, in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, near modern Finiq in southern Albania. It was the chief town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaonians">Chaonians</a> and a frequent meeting place of the Epirote League. Illyrian raiders sent by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teuta">Teuta</a> sacked it in 230 BCE, and in 205 BCE it gave its name to the peace that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-macedonian-war">First Macedonian War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phoenice.jpg" alt="Phoenice" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robmor01 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2nd_c._BC_shops.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earthquake of 551 CE destroyed most of the cities of <b>Phoenice</b>, and in 636 the Muslim conquest of the Levant took the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>They formed a federal state, the Koinon of the Epirotes, whose meetings were often held at <b>Phoenice</b> in Chaonia, near the Ionian coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/epirote-league">Epirote League</a></li></ul></section>
